eval("__webpack_require__.r(__webpack_exports__);\n/* harmony export */ __webpack_require__.d(__webpack_exports__, {\n/* harmony export */   news: () => (/* binding */ news)\n/* harmony export */ });\nconst news = [\n    {\n        id: \"n1\",\n        title: '\"AI Has Made Me a Better Teacher, Not a Lazier One\" — One Teacher\\'s Story',\n        excerpt: \"Sarah, an EFL teacher in Barcelona, shares how she went from sceptic to advocate after using AI to personalise materials for her mixed-ability class of adult learners.\",\n        category: \"interview\",\n        date: \"March 2025\",\n        author: \"ESLteacher.ai Team\",\n        coverImage: \"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1573496359142-b8d87734a5a2?w=1200&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format\",\n        body: `When Sarah first heard colleagues talking about using ChatGPT to write lesson plans, she rolled her eyes. \"I thought it was just another EdTech fad,\" she admits. \"And honestly, I worried it would make teachers lazy — or worse, replaceable.\" Eighteen months later, she's one of the most vocal AI advocates in her school.\n\nSarah teaches Business English to adult professionals at a language academy in Barcelona. Her classes are notoriously mixed — she might have a B1 accountant sitting next to a C1 marketing director, both expecting material that challenges them at their level. It was this challenge, more than anything else, that pushed her to experiment with AI.\n\n\"The breakthrough came when I started using AI to differentiate a single activity,\" she explains. \"I'd write one task and then ask Claude to give me three versions — one for B1, one for B2, one for C1. What used to take me an hour took me ten minutes. And the quality was genuinely good.\"\n\nShe's quick to add that AI hasn't replaced her professional judgement. \"I always review and edit what it produces. I change the vocabulary, I adjust the context to match my specific students. The AI gives me a first draft, not a finished product.\" She estimates she spends around 40% less time on materials preparation now — time she reinvests in actual lesson delivery and student feedback.\n\nHer advice to sceptical colleagues: \"Start small. Don't try to overhaul your whole teaching practice. Just pick one painful task — the thing you dread preparing every week — and try using AI for that. Once you see it working, you'll find your own way forward.\"`\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"n2\",\n        title: \"The Ethics of AI in Language Teaching: What Every Teacher Should Know\",\n        excerpt: \"As AI becomes standard in many classrooms, ELT professionals are debating questions of authorship, student autonomy, and whether AI-generated content undermines learning. Here's a balanced overview.\",\n        category: \"ethics\",\n        date: \"February 2025\",\n        author: \"ESLteacher.ai Team\",\n        coverImage: \"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1434030216411-0b793f4b4173?w=1200&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format\",\n        body: `The rapid adoption of AI in English language teaching has opened up genuine pedagogical opportunities — but it has also raised questions that the profession hasn't fully answered yet. Questions about authorship, about the nature of language learning, and about what we owe our students when we bring these tools into the classroom.\n\nThe most immediate concern is authenticity. Language learning requires practice — and practice requires effort. When AI generates a writing task and then also provides a model answer, are students actually engaging with the language, or are they becoming skilled at operating a machine? Many teachers argue that the skill of prompting AI is itself a form of language use. Others aren't so sure.\n\nThen there's the question of data and privacy. Most mainstream AI tools — ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini — are cloud-based, which means student interactions and data may be stored and processed by third parties. Before using these tools in class, teachers should check their institution's data protection policy and, where relevant, obtain appropriate consent.\n\nThere are also equity concerns. AI tools are unevenly distributed. Students with reliable devices, fast internet, and premium subscriptions will get more out of these tools than those without. A classroom that assumes AI access may inadvertently disadvantage its most vulnerable learners.\n\nNone of this means AI should be avoided. But it does mean teachers need to approach it thoughtfully. The most ethical use of AI in ELT is transparent: telling students when and how you're using it, teaching them to evaluate AI output critically, and making sure the technology serves learning rather than replacing it.`\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"n3\",\n        title: \"5 Ways Teachers Are Using AI for Classroom Practice Right Now\",\n        excerpt: \"From instant feedback to differentiated reading texts, we surveyed 200 ESL teachers to find out exactly how they're integrating AI into daily lessons — and what's working.\",\n        category: \"classroom\",\n        date: \"March 2025\",\n        author: \"ESLteacher.ai Team\",\n        coverImage: \"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1580582932707-520aed937b7b?w=1200&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format\",\n        body: `We asked 200 English language teachers how they're actually using AI in their classrooms — not how they think they should be using it, but what they're doing right now, every week. The results were more varied and more practical than we expected.\n\nThe most common use, cited by 68% of respondents, was generating differentiated materials. Teachers described using AI to take a single reading text or grammar exercise and produce multiple versions at different CEFR levels — allowing them to run mixed-ability classes without preparing entirely separate lessons.\n\nThe second most common use (54%) was writing feedback. Rather than commenting on every student essay themselves, many teachers are now using AI to generate a first round of language-focused feedback, which they then review and personalise. \"It catches things I might miss when I'm tired,\" said one respondent. \"And it frees me up to focus on content and ideas rather than grammar.\"\n\nThird on the list (47%) was conversation practice. Several teachers described setting up AI chatbots for students to practise speaking topics between lessons — especially useful for one-to-one students who don't have English-speaking environments outside class.\n\nComing in fourth (39%) was lesson planning. Teachers reported using AI to generate activity sequences, warm-up ideas, and discussion questions — treating it as a \"brainstorming partner\" rather than a plan generator.\n\nFinally, 31% reported using AI for assessment design: creating quiz questions, gap-fill exercises, and rubrics that they then adapted for their specific classes. The consistent theme across all five uses was that AI works best when teachers remain in editorial control — generating options rather than final outputs.`\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"n4\",\n        title: \"Google, OpenAI, and Anthropic: Which AI Is Best for Teachers?\",\n        excerpt: \"We compare ChatGPT, Gemini, and Claude on the tasks teachers actually care about: generating lesson plans, giving writing feedback, and creating exam practice materials.\",\n        category: \"news\",\n        date: \"January 2025\",\n        author: \"ESLteacher.ai Team\",\n        coverImage: \"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1677442135703-1787eea5ce01?w=1200&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format\",\n        body: `Three AI platforms now dominate the conversation in ELT: ChatGPT (OpenAI), Claude (Anthropic), and Gemini (Google). Each has genuine strengths for teachers — and each has limitations that matter in a classroom context. Here's what we found after testing all three on real teaching tasks.\n\nOn lesson planning, all three performed well, but with different strengths. ChatGPT tends to produce more structured, template-style lesson plans with clear timing and objectives. Claude produces more nuanced plans that feel closer to how an experienced teacher actually thinks — it's more likely to anticipate pedagogical issues and suggest alternatives. Gemini's strength is integration: if you're already in the Google ecosystem, it connects naturally with Docs, Slides, and Classroom.\n\nFor writing feedback, Claude is the standout performer. Its feedback is detailed, specific, and — crucially — kind in tone. It's less likely to produce the kind of blunt, discouraging comments that can demotivate language learners. ChatGPT is also strong here, especially with grammar. Gemini lagged behind on nuance, though it has improved significantly in recent months.\n\nOn exam material creation — gap fills, multiple choice, IELTS-style questions — ChatGPT is arguably the most reliable. It handles structured output formats consistently and produces well-calibrated difficulty levels when prompted correctly.\n\nOur overall recommendation for most teachers: start with Claude or ChatGPT, depending on whether you want more nuanced prose output (Claude) or more reliable structured formats (ChatGPT). Use Gemini if you're deeply embedded in Google Workspace. All three offer free tiers that are sufficient for most classroom use.`\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"n5\",\n        title: \"Should Students Be Allowed to Use AI for Homework? The Debate Continues\",\n        excerpt: \"Teachers are divided. Some see AI as a legitimate learning tool; others worry about academic integrity. We hear from both sides and outline a practical middle-ground approach.\",\n        category: \"ethics\",\n        date: \"February 2025\",\n        author: \"ESLteacher.ai Team\",\n        coverImage: \"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1456513080510-7bf3a84b82f8?w=1200&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format\",\n        body: `Few questions divide English language teachers more sharply right now than this one: should students be allowed — or even encouraged — to use AI for their homework? The debate cuts across every level of the profession, from primary school teachers to university lecturers, and there are no easy answers.\n\nThose who support student AI use argue that it reflects the real world. In most professional environments, AI assistance is not only permitted — it's expected. Preparing students to work effectively with these tools is part of preparing them for life after education. They also argue that a student who uses AI to get a first draft and then revises and personalises it is still engaging meaningfully with the language.\n\nThose who oppose it — or who support tighter restrictions — make an equally compelling case. Language learning is fundamentally about building internal competence: the ability to produce and process language in real time, without assistance. If students outsource the production of language to a machine, they may pass their homework but fail to develop the underlying skills that actually matter. \"My job isn't to get students to produce a correct piece of writing,\" said one teacher. \"My job is to develop the cognitive and linguistic processes that make them capable writers.\"\n\nA practical middle ground is emerging in many institutions. Teachers are redesigning homework to focus on process rather than product — asking students to submit AI drafts alongside their own revisions, or to annotate AI output explaining what they changed and why. Others are moving high-stakes writing back into the classroom, where AI use is not possible, while keeping homework as a lower-stakes space for exploration.\n\nWhatever position schools take, transparency is essential. Students need to know clearly what is and isn't permitted — and teachers need to be able to have honest, non-punitive conversations about how these tools are being used.`\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"n6\",\n        title: \"How One School District Used AI to Reduce Teacher Workload by 30%\",\n        excerpt: \"A case study from a New Zealand secondary school that piloted AI-assisted lesson planning and marking support. The results — and the unexpected challenges — are worth reading.\",\n        category: \"classroom\",\n        date: \"January 2025\",\n        author: \"ESLteacher.ai Team\",\n        coverImage: \"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1509062522246-3755977927d7?w=1200&h=500&fit=crop&auto=format\",\n        body: `When Wellington College introduced an AI pilot programme for its English department in mid-2024, the goal was modest: reduce the time teachers spent on routine tasks without affecting the quality of student experience. Eight months later, the results exceeded expectations — but not without some surprises along the way.\n\nThe pilot involved twelve English and ESOL teachers using a combination of Claude and a custom-built school prompt library to support lesson planning, differentiation, and written feedback. Teachers kept time logs for the first three months, tracking how long specific tasks took before and after AI assistance was introduced.\n\nThe headline figure — a 30% reduction in total preparation time — came primarily from two sources: differentiated materials (where teachers previously prepared separate resources for different ability groups) and written feedback on student work (where AI produced a first draft that teachers then reviewed and personalised, typically in a fraction of the original time).\n\nBut the pilot also revealed challenges that the team hadn't fully anticipated. Some teachers reported an initial increase in workload as they learned to write effective prompts and develop a reliable workflow. \"There was a learning curve,\" said the department head. \"For the first month, some teachers felt like it was taking more time, not less. It was only once they'd found their rhythm that the time savings kicked in.\"\n\nThere were also unexpected pedagogical shifts. Several teachers reported becoming more ambitious with differentiation — offering more ability levels, more task variety — because AI made it feasible. \"I started doing things I'd always wanted to do but never had time for,\" said one teacher. The implication is significant: AI may not just save time, but change what teachers consider possible.`\n    }\n];\n//# sourceURL=[module]\n//# sourceMappingURL=data:application/json;charset=utf-8;base64,\n//# sourceURL=webpack-internal:///(rsc)/./data/news.ts\n");

