eval("__webpack_require__.r(__webpack_exports__);\n/* harmony export */ __webpack_require__.d(__webpack_exports__, {\n/* harmony export */   prompts: () => (/* binding */ prompts)\n/* harmony export */ });\nconst prompts = [\n    {\n        id: \"p1\",\n        title: \"Two Truths and a Lie Warmer\",\n        preview: \"Generate 3 sets of 'Two Truths and a Lie' statements around a topic students are studying.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are a creative ESL teacher. Generate 3 sets of \"Two Truths and a Lie\" statements for an English class currently studying the topic of [TOPIC]. Each set should include 2 true statements and 1 false statement. The statements should be at [CEFR LEVEL] level, interesting, and spark conversation. Format each set clearly and include a brief explanation of why the lie is false. Target age group: [AGE GROUP].`,\n        category: \"Warmers\",\n        level: \"A2–C1\",\n        time: \"10 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"warmer\",\n            \"speaking\",\n            \"fun\",\n            \"all levels\"\n        ],\n        likes: 214,\n        exampleOutput: `Set 1 (Topic: Technology)\\n• True: The first smartphone was released in 2007.\\n• True: There are more mobile phones than people on Earth.\\n• Lie: The internet was invented in 1969. (This was ARPANET, not the public internet)\\n\\nGreat for getting students talking about tech vocabulary!`,\n        classroomUse: \"Share each set one at a time. Students vote on which statement is the lie, then discuss in pairs before revealing the answer. Works great as a 10-minute session opener.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Beginner version: use very simple, factual statements about daily life\",\n            \"IELTS version: use academic topics like climate, health, or technology\",\n            \"Online class version: use a poll tool like Mentimeter for students to vote\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p2\",\n        title: \"Speaking Discussion Questions Generator\",\n        preview: \"Create a set of graded discussion questions on any topic for a speaking lesson.\",\n        fullPrompt: `Act as an experienced ESL speaking teacher. Generate 8 discussion questions on the topic of [TOPIC] suitable for [CEFR LEVEL] learners. Organise the questions from easier (factual, personal) to more challenging (opinion, hypothetical). Include 2 follow-up prompts for each question to help extend the conversation. The questions should feel natural and engaging, not like a test. For class context: [CLASS TYPE, e.g. adult general English / exam prep / business English].`,\n        category: \"Speaking\",\n        level: \"B1–C1\",\n        time: \"20 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"speaking\",\n            \"discussion\",\n            \"fluency\",\n            \"opinion\"\n        ],\n        likes: 189,\n        exampleOutput: `Topic: Social Media\\n1. How much time do you spend on social media each day? → Follow-up: Has that changed over the last few years? Why?\\n2. Which platform do you use most and why? → Follow-up: What would you miss most if it disappeared?\\n...and 6 more graded questions with follow-ups.`,\n        classroomUse: \"Print or share on screen. Students discuss in pairs for 15 minutes, then regroup to share interesting responses with the class. Great for B1+ groups.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"IELTS Part 3 style: more abstract and analytical questions\",\n            \"Beginner version: yes/no questions with simple follow-ups\",\n            \"Business English: adapt topic to professional contexts\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p3\",\n        title: \"Grammar Error Correction Worksheet\",\n        preview: \"Generate a grammar error correction exercise targeting a specific grammar point.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are an EFL grammar teacher. Create a grammar error correction exercise for [CEFR LEVEL] students focusing on [GRAMMAR POINT, e.g. present perfect vs. past simple]. Write 10 sentences, each containing exactly one grammatical error related to the target grammar point. Use natural, contextually rich sentences — avoid isolated, artificial examples. After the exercise, provide an answer key with a brief explanation for each correction. Topic theme for the sentences: [TOPIC].`,\n        category: \"Grammar\",\n        level: \"A2–B2\",\n        time: \"15 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"grammar\",\n            \"error correction\",\n            \"worksheet\",\n            \"accuracy\"\n        ],\n        likes: 156,\n        exampleOutput: `Target: Present Perfect vs. Past Simple\\n1. \"I have seen that film last week.\" → Correction: \"I saw that film last week.\" (past simple needed — specific past time reference)\\n2. \"She already finished her homework.\" → \"She has already finished her homework.\" (present perfect — result relevant now)\\n...`,\n        classroomUse: \"Give students 10 minutes to find and correct the errors individually, then compare with a partner before whole-class feedback. Pairs can also try to explain why each correction was needed.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Intermediate challenge: include distractors with correct sentences among the errors\",\n            \"Writing focus: use paragraph format instead of isolated sentences\",\n            \"Peer correction: students create their own error sentences for classmates\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p4\",\n        title: \"IELTS Speaking Part 2 Cue Card\",\n        preview: \"Generate a realistic IELTS Speaking Part 2 cue card with model answer notes.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are an IELTS examiner and speaking coach. Create a realistic IELTS Speaking Part 2 task card on the topic of [TOPIC]. The card should follow the official IELTS format: a main prompt starting with \"Describe...\" followed by 3–4 bullet point prompts and a closing \"You should say:\" line. After the card, provide: (1) a list of 8–10 useful vocabulary items with definitions, (2) a brief model answer outline (not a full script) showing how a band 7+ response might be structured, and (3) 2 common mistakes to avoid.`,\n        category: \"IELTS\",\n        level: \"B2–C1\",\n        time: \"20 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"IELTS\",\n            \"speaking\",\n            \"part 2\",\n            \"exam prep\",\n            \"cue card\"\n        ],\n        likes: 298,\n        exampleOutput: `Describe a time when you helped someone.\\nYou should say:\\n• who you helped\\n• what the situation was\\n• how you helped them\\nAnd explain how you felt afterwards.\\n\\nVocabulary: lend a hand, come to someone's rescue, volunteer, go out of your way...`,\n        classroomUse: \"Give students 1 minute to prepare, then have them speak for 1–2 minutes. Peer listeners use a simple checklist to note vocabulary range and fluency. Debrief with the model outline.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Band 6 model: show a mid-range answer with development suggestions\",\n            \"Online class: use breakout rooms for simultaneous practice\",\n            \"Self-study: students record themselves and compare to the model\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p5\",\n        title: \"Vocabulary Pictionary Cards\",\n        preview: \"Turn a vocabulary list into a ready-to-play Pictionary game for your class.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are a creative ESL teacher designing vocabulary activities. I have a list of vocabulary items: [VOCABULARY LIST]. Create a classroom Pictionary game using these words. For each word: (1) confirm it is drawable/visualisable, (2) write a simple definition students can use as a hint if needed, and (3) rate the drawing difficulty (Easy / Medium / Hard). Organise the cards into teams of 4–6. Include brief instructions for running the game with [CLASS SIZE] students at [CEFR LEVEL]. Suggest 2 variations to make it more or less challenging.`,\n        category: \"Vocabulary\",\n        level: \"A1–B2\",\n        time: \"20 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"vocabulary\",\n            \"game\",\n            \"speaking\",\n            \"fun\",\n            \"visual\"\n        ],\n        likes: 173,\n        exampleOutput: `Word: \"exhausted\"\\nDefinition hint: feeling extremely tired, no energy left\\nDifficulty: Medium (can be shown through posture, drooping eyes)\\n\\nWord: \"magnificent\"\\nDifficulty: Hard — suggest using \"amazing building\" as the visual cue`,\n        classroomUse: \"Divide class into teams of 4–5. One student draws while teammates guess in English only. Set a 60-second timer. Rotate drawers each round. Award points for correct guesses.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Collocations version: students must use the word in a sentence after guessing\",\n            \"Digital version: use Skribbl.io for online classes\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p6\",\n        title: \"Reading Comprehension Question Set\",\n        preview: \"Generate a full set of reading comprehension questions for any text or topic.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are an experienced ESL reading teacher. Based on the following text (or topic if no text is provided): [PASTE TEXT OR DESCRIBE TOPIC], create a complete set of reading comprehension questions for [CEFR LEVEL] students. Include: (1) 4 True/False/Not Given questions, (2) 4 multiple-choice questions with 3 options each, (3) 3 short-answer questions that require inference, (4) 1 vocabulary-in-context question. Provide a full answer key. If no text is provided, generate a short 200-word reading passage first, then create the questions.`,\n        category: \"Reading\",\n        level: \"A2–C1\",\n        time: \"25 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"reading\",\n            \"comprehension\",\n            \"worksheet\",\n            \"skills\"\n        ],\n        likes: 134,\n        exampleOutput: `Passage generated on: The History of Coffee\\n\\nTrue/False/Not Given:\\n1. Coffee was first grown in Brazil. → False (Ethiopia)\\n2. Caffeine is found in tea as well as coffee. → True\\n...\\nShort-answer Q: Why do you think coffee became so popular in Europe during the 17th century? (Inference required)`,\n        classroomUse: \"Students read independently, then complete questions. Pair check before whole-class feedback. Use inference questions as discussion springboards for higher levels.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"IELTS Academic: use TFNG format throughout and include a heading-matching task\",\n            \"Lower levels: reduce word count and focus on True/False only\",\n            \"Flipped: give questions before reading to set a reading purpose\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p7\",\n        title: \"Listening Follow-Up Activity Pack\",\n        preview: \"Create discussion, vocabulary, and writing follow-up tasks for any listening topic.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are an ESL teacher who has just used a listening activity with your class on the topic of [TOPIC]. The listening was at [CEFR LEVEL]. Design a complete follow-up activity pack including: (1) 3 discussion questions related to the listening topic, (2) a vocabulary focus section with 5 key words/phrases likely heard in the audio, including definitions and example sentences, (3) a short writing task (50–80 words) that connects to the listening theme, and (4) one extension activity for fast finishers.`,\n        category: \"Listening\",\n        level: \"B1–C1\",\n        time: \"20 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"listening\",\n            \"follow-up\",\n            \"vocabulary\",\n            \"writing\"\n        ],\n        likes: 112,\n        exampleOutput: `Topic: A podcast about remote work\\n\\nDiscussion Q1: Would you prefer to work from home or in an office? Why?\\nVocabulary: \"flexible hours\" — having the freedom to choose when you work\\nWriting task: Write a short message to your manager explaining why you'd like to work from home two days a week.`,\n        classroomUse: \"Use immediately after the listening. Discussion in pairs (5 min), vocabulary matching activity (5 min), writing task individually (10 min), then share responses.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Exam class: replace discussion with a speaking task timed to simulate exam conditions\",\n            \"Beginner: simplify vocabulary section with picture prompts\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p8\",\n        title: \"Full Lesson Plan Generator\",\n        preview: \"Generate a complete, ready-to-teach lesson plan from a topic and level.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are a professional ESL curriculum designer. Create a full 60-minute lesson plan for [CEFR LEVEL] learners on the topic of [TOPIC]. The lesson should follow this structure: (1) Warmer / Lead-in (5 min), (2) Vocabulary pre-teaching (10 min), (3) Main activity — reading or listening (15 min), (4) Language focus — grammar or vocabulary in context (15 min), (5) Speaking or writing task (10 min), (6) Wrap-up and feedback (5 min). For each stage, include: the activity description, teacher instructions, materials needed, and what students produce. Learning objective: by the end of the lesson, students will be able to [SKILL/LANGUAGE POINT].`,\n        category: \"Lesson Planning\",\n        level: \"A1–C2\",\n        time: \"60 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"lesson plan\",\n            \"planning\",\n            \"full lesson\",\n            \"professional\"\n        ],\n        likes: 267,\n        exampleOutput: `Topic: Giving Advice (Modal Verbs) — Level: B1\\nObjective: Students will be able to give advice using should, ought to, and if I were you...\\nStage 1 — Warmer (5 min): Students rank 5 common life problems in order of seriousness...\\nStage 2 — Vocabulary (10 min): Pre-teach: dilemma, perspective, weigh up options...`,\n        classroomUse: \"Use as a complete teaching guide. Adapt timings to your class. The lesson plan can be shared with a co-teacher or used for lesson observation preparation.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"90-minute version: add a second main activity or extended project task\",\n            \"Online class: include Breakout room tasks and screen-share activities\",\n            \"Young learners: add a game or song for each main stage\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p9\",\n        title: \"PowerPoint Slide Design Prompt\",\n        preview: \"Get a detailed brief to create a visual, engaging PPT for any lesson topic.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are a visual learning designer specialising in ESL education. Create a detailed brief for a PowerPoint presentation for an English class lesson on [TOPIC] at [CEFR LEVEL]. For each slide, specify: the slide title, the main visual description (what image or graphic should appear), the text content (minimal — max 20 words per slide), and any animation or interaction suggestion. The presentation should have: 1 title slide, 1 learning objective slide, 4–6 content slides, 1 activity slide, and 1 summary/reflection slide. Design principle: visual-first, minimal text, teacher-led discussion prompts on speaker notes.`,\n        category: \"PPT Design\",\n        level: \"A1–C2\",\n        time: \"30 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"presentation\",\n            \"PowerPoint\",\n            \"visual\",\n            \"lesson design\"\n        ],\n        likes: 145,\n        exampleOutput: `Slide 3 — \"What is Climate Change?\"\\nVisual: Split image — healthy forest vs. dried landscape\\nText: \"The Earth is warming. Why?\"\\nSpeaker note: Ask students what differences they notice before explaining the causes.\\nAnimation: Fade in image halves one by one.`,\n        classroomUse: \"Use the brief as a prompt in Canva, Google Slides, or PowerPoint. Paste the brief for each slide into your design tool to build the presentation systematically.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Student project version: students each design 2 slides on an assigned subtopic\",\n            \"Minimalist version: 5 slides maximum, one key idea per slide\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p10\",\n        title: \"Writing Task Feedback Generator\",\n        preview: \"Paste a student's writing and get structured, constructive feedback in seconds.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are an experienced ESL writing teacher. A student at [CEFR LEVEL] has submitted the following piece of writing. The task was: [TASK DESCRIPTION]. Student's writing: [PASTE STUDENT TEXT HERE]. Please provide structured feedback covering: (1) Task achievement — did the student complete the task? (2) Coherence and cohesion — is it well-organised and connected? (3) Grammar — note 2–3 key patterns to focus on with example corrections, (4) Vocabulary — highlight 2 strong word choices and suggest 2 upgrades, (5) One clear priority for improvement. Use encouraging, constructive language. End with a suggested band/grade if appropriate.`,\n        category: \"Writing\",\n        level: \"A2–C2\",\n        time: \"10 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"writing\",\n            \"feedback\",\n            \"correction\",\n            \"assessment\"\n        ],\n        likes: 203,\n        exampleOutput: `Task Achievement: The student addressed the main point clearly. ✓\\nCoherence: Good paragraph structure, though transitions between ideas could be smoother.\\nGrammar focus: Article usage (a/the) — \"I went to hospital\" should be \"I went to the hospital\" in British English.\\nVocabulary highlight: \"significant impact\" — excellent academic phrase.\\nPriority: Work on sentence variety — most sentences follow the same Subject-Verb-Object pattern.`,\n        classroomUse: \"Paste student texts one by one. Use the feedback for 1:1 conferences or print and annotate. Can also be shown anonymously to the whole class as a teaching example.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"IELTS version: request feedback framed around the 4 official IELTS marking criteria\",\n            \"Peer feedback: give students the prompt structure to evaluate each other's work\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p11\",\n        title: \"Vocabulary Review Kahoot-Style Quiz\",\n        preview: \"Generate a 10-question vocabulary quiz with answer options, ready for any platform.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are an ESL vocabulary teacher. Create a 10-question multiple-choice vocabulary quiz for [CEFR LEVEL] students based on the following word list (or topic): [WORD LIST / TOPIC]. For each question: write a clear, natural sentence using the target word with a gap, provide 4 answer options (1 correct, 3 plausible distractors), and briefly explain why the correct answer is right. Format the output so it can be easily entered into Kahoot, Google Forms, or Quizlet. Also include a scoring guide and a suggested follow-up activity.`,\n        category: \"Vocabulary\",\n        level: \"A1–B2\",\n        time: \"15 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"vocabulary\",\n            \"quiz\",\n            \"kahoot\",\n            \"review\",\n            \"digital\"\n        ],\n        likes: 187,\n        exampleOutput: `Q3: She felt _______ after running the marathon. (exhausted / bored / excited / confused)\\nAnswer: exhausted ✓\\nExplanation: \"Exhausted\" means extremely tired — the most logical state after running 42km.\\nDistractors: \"bored\" and \"confused\" are clearly wrong in context; \"excited\" is a common confusion for false beginners.`,\n        classroomUse: \"Enter questions into Kahoot for a 10-minute review game. Alternatively, use as a printed quiz. Follow up by having students write original sentences with any words they missed.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Collocations focus: test word pairs rather than single words\",\n            \"Picture-based: replace the sentence with an image description for lower levels\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p12\",\n        title: \"Homework Task Designer\",\n        preview: \"Design a meaningful, low-prep homework task that connects to your lesson.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are an ESL teacher who wants to assign homework that is engaging, achievable, and connects to the class lesson. Today's lesson topic was: [TOPIC]. Students are at [CEFR LEVEL]. Design a homework task that: takes no more than [TIME, e.g. 20 minutes] to complete, extends the lesson's main language point, requires students to use English authentically (not just fill gaps), and can be self-checked or easily reviewed by the teacher. Include the task instructions for students, the language focus, what the teacher needs to prepare (if anything), and a success criteria students can use to check their own work.`,\n        category: \"Homework\",\n        level: \"A2–C1\",\n        time: \"20 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"homework\",\n            \"planning\",\n            \"extension\",\n            \"autonomous learning\"\n        ],\n        likes: 98,\n        exampleOutput: `Lesson topic: Describing trends (graphs and data)\\nHomework: Find one chart or graph online (news site, social media). Write 5 sentences describing the trend using vocabulary from today: rose sharply, declined steadily, peaked at, levelled off.\\nSuccess criteria: All 5 sentences use different trend vocabulary. No copying from the source text.`,\n        classroomUse: \"Share task via Google Classroom or print. At the start of next class, students share their graphs and sentences in pairs — instant review activity built in.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Digital version: students post their work to a shared Padlet or Google Slides deck\",\n            \"Speaking homework: students record a 1-minute voice note using the target language\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p13\",\n        title: \"Energiser: Word Association Race\",\n        preview: \"Run a fast-paced word association game to boost energy mid-class.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are a dynamic ESL teacher who wants to energise a class that is losing focus. Create a Word Association Race activity for [CLASS SIZE] students at [CEFR LEVEL]. The theme is [TOPIC or 'any vocabulary from recent lessons']. Provide: (1) a set of 5 starter words, (2) clear rules for the race (time limit, scoring, valid/invalid answers), (3) a variation that makes it harder for advanced students, and (4) a debrief question to connect it back to the lesson. The whole activity should take no more than 8 minutes.`,\n        category: \"Energisers\",\n        level: \"A2–C1\",\n        time: \"8 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"energiser\",\n            \"vocabulary\",\n            \"speaking\",\n            \"game\",\n            \"fast\"\n        ],\n        likes: 142,\n        exampleOutput: `Starter word: \"TRAVEL\"\\nRound 1: Students shout associated words as fast as possible (airport, suitcase, holiday...)\\nScoring: 1 point per word, 0 for repeats, -1 for non-English.\\nAdvanced variation: Each word must be a different part of speech from the previous one.\\nDebrief: \"Which word surprised you? Did any remind you of vocabulary from last week?\"`,\n        classroomUse: \"No materials needed. Stand students up if possible. Run 2–3 rounds with different starter words. Takes 5–8 minutes and leaves students re-energised and engaged.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Slower pace: students write associations individually first, then compare\",\n            \"Categories: restrict associations to a specific word class (nouns only, verbs only)\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p14\",\n        title: \"Conditional Sentence Builder\",\n        preview: \"Create a structured activity to practise all conditional forms in context.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are a grammar teacher specialising in conditionals. Create a complete activity set for [CEFR LEVEL] students to practise [CONDITIONAL TYPE: 0, 1st, 2nd, 3rd, or mixed]. Include: (1) a brief explanation of form and use (2–3 sentences, no heavy grammar metalanguage), (2) 8 sentence-completion tasks using realistic, interesting contexts, (3) a follow-up speaking activity where students personalise the grammar point, and (4) 3 common errors with corrections. Context/topic for the sentences: [TOPIC].`,\n        category: \"Grammar\",\n        level: \"B1–C1\",\n        time: \"25 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"grammar\",\n            \"conditionals\",\n            \"accuracy\",\n            \"speaking\"\n        ],\n        likes: 167,\n        exampleOutput: `2nd Conditional focus:\\n\"If I _______ (be) the school director, I _______ (change) the timetable.\"\\nSpeaking: Tell your partner 3 things you would do if you had unlimited money for your classroom.\\nError: \"If I would have more time, I would study more.\" → Correct: \"If I had more time, I would study more.\"`,\n        classroomUse: \"Completion task individually (10 min), then speaking in pairs (10 min). Teacher monitors for accuracy during pairs, then conducts whole-class feedback on common errors heard.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Mixed conditionals: include examples that blend 2nd and 3rd conditionals\",\n            \"Beginner-friendly: focus on 1st conditional only with real-world outcomes\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p15\",\n        title: \"Assessment Rubric Creator\",\n        preview: \"Generate a clear, student-friendly marking rubric for any task type.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are an assessment specialist in English language teaching. Create a marking rubric for a [TASK TYPE, e.g. oral presentation / essay / role play] at [CEFR LEVEL]. The rubric should: have 4–5 clear criteria (e.g. content, language accuracy, fluency, pronunciation), use 4 performance levels (Excellent / Good / Developing / Needs support), include brief descriptors for each criterion at each level, be written in language students can understand, and align with [EXAM OR CONTEXT, e.g. Cambridge B2, general progress assessment]. Also include a teacher-facing scoring guide and a student self-assessment version.`,\n        category: \"Assessment\",\n        level: \"A2–C2\",\n        time: \"15 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"assessment\",\n            \"rubric\",\n            \"marking\",\n            \"evaluation\",\n            \"speaking\"\n        ],\n        likes: 176,\n        exampleOutput: `Criterion: Vocabulary Range\\nExcellent: Uses a wide range of accurate, topic-specific vocabulary with very few errors.\\nGood: Uses appropriate vocabulary with occasional repetition or minor inaccuracies.\\nDeveloping: Limited vocabulary range; relies on basic words and some L1 interference.\\nNeeds support: Very limited vocabulary makes communication difficult.`,\n        classroomUse: \"Share with students before the task so they understand expectations. Use as a self-assessment tool after practice runs. Teacher marks using the same rubric for consistency.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"IELTS-aligned: map criteria to Task Achievement, Coherence, Lexical Resource, Grammatical Range\",\n            \"Peer assessment: students use the rubric to evaluate a classmate's work\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p16\",\n        title: \"IELTS Writing Task 2 Outline Generator\",\n        preview: \"Get a structured essay outline for any IELTS Writing Task 2 question.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are an IELTS writing coach. A student has been given this Writing Task 2 question: [PASTE QUESTION]. Create a complete essay outline that: clearly identifies the question type (opinion / discuss both views / problem-solution / advantage-disadvantage), proposes a clear thesis statement, outlines each paragraph with a topic sentence, 2 supporting points, and an example for each, and includes linking language suggestions for each paragraph. Then highlight 6 topic-specific vocabulary items the student should aim to include. Finally, note 2 common pitfalls for this question type.`,\n        category: \"IELTS\",\n        level: \"B2–C1\",\n        time: \"15 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"IELTS\",\n            \"writing\",\n            \"task 2\",\n            \"essay\",\n            \"exam prep\"\n        ],\n        likes: 234,\n        exampleOutput: `Question: \"Some people believe that universities should focus on employability skills rather than academic knowledge. To what extent do you agree?\"\\n\\nType: Opinion essay\\nThesis: While practical skills are valuable, a balanced approach combining academic and vocational training best prepares graduates.\\n\\nParagraph 2 (Support employability view):\\nTopic sentence: Employers increasingly value practical, job-ready skills over theoretical knowledge...`,\n        classroomUse: \"Students receive the outline and use it to write a timed essay (40 min). Alternatively, show the outline and discuss structure before students create their own from a different question.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Band 7+ model: after the outline, generate a full model answer for comparison\",\n            \"Student-generated: students create the outline first, then compare to AI output\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p17\",\n        title: \"Role Play Scenario Cards\",\n        preview: \"Create realistic role play scenarios to practise functional language.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are an ESL speaking teacher. Create 4 realistic role play scenario cards for [CEFR LEVEL] students to practise the functional language of [FUNCTION, e.g. making complaints / giving advice / negotiating / job interviews]. Each card should include: a realistic setting and context, one card for Student A and one for Student B with their role and goal, key phrases they might need (3–5 per card), and a language focus box with the target structure. The scenarios should be appropriate for [CONTEXT: adult general English / business English / academic English / young learners].`,\n        category: \"Speaking\",\n        level: \"B1–C1\",\n        time: \"20 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"speaking\",\n            \"role play\",\n            \"functional language\",\n            \"communication\"\n        ],\n        likes: 158,\n        exampleOutput: `Scenario: Returning a faulty product\\nStudent A (Customer): You bought a laptop 3 weeks ago. The screen has stopped working. You want a full refund.\\nStudent B (Shop assistant): Your policy allows exchange within 30 days but not a refund after 2 weeks.\\nKey phrases A: \"I'd like to return...\", \"I'm afraid this isn't acceptable...\"\\nKey phrases B: \"I understand your frustration...\", \"What I can offer you is...\"`,\n        classroomUse: \"Give each student their card only. Allow 2–3 minutes to prepare, then role play for 5 minutes. Swap roles or scenarios. Teacher monitors for target language use.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Video version: students film the role play for self-review\",\n            \"Extension: students rewrite the scenario with a different outcome\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    },\n    {\n        id: \"p18\",\n        title: \"Paragraph Writing Scaffold\",\n        preview: \"Provide a structured scaffold to help students write a clear, well-developed paragraph.\",\n        fullPrompt: `You are an ESL writing teacher working with students who are learning to write structured paragraphs. Create a complete paragraph writing scaffold for [CEFR LEVEL] students on the topic of [TOPIC]. The scaffold should include: (1) a paragraph frame with sentence starters (Topic sentence: \"One important reason why ___...\"), (2) a model paragraph at band/grade level for reference, (3) a checklist students use to self-assess their paragraph, and (4) 3 sentence-combining exercises to improve cohesion. Target skill: [e.g. argument paragraphs / descriptive paragraphs / compare-contrast].`,\n        category: \"Writing\",\n        level: \"A2–B2\",\n        time: \"20 min\",\n        tags: [\n            \"writing\",\n            \"scaffold\",\n            \"paragraph\",\n            \"structure\",\n            \"beginner-friendly\"\n        ],\n        likes: 121,\n        exampleOutput: `Topic: The benefits of learning a second language\\nScaffold:\\nTopic sentence: \"One significant advantage of learning a second language is ___.\"\\nExample: \"For instance, research shows that bilingual people ___...\"\\nConclusion: \"This suggests that ___...\"\\n\\nChecklist: ✓ Does my paragraph have a clear topic sentence? ✓ Have I given a specific example?`,\n        classroomUse: \"Distribute the scaffold as a printed or digital template. Students write first attempt with the frame, then try again without it. Compare the two attempts to see how structure improved.\",\n        variations: [\n            \"Academic writing: focus on PEEL or TEEL paragraph structure\",\n            \"Beginner: reduce to 3-sentence scaffold (Point, Example, Feeling)\"\n        ],\n        worksWith: [\n            \"ChatGPT\",\n            \"Claude\",\n            \"Gemini\"\n        ]\n    }\n];\n//# sourceURL=[module]\n//# sourceMappingURL=data:application/json;charset=utf-8;base64,\n//# sourceURL=webpack-internal:///(rsc)/./data/prompts.ts\n");

